      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Are waterproof socks worth buying?",
            "answer": "<p>If your feet get wet on a regular basis — a job, a commute, a weekly walk, daily wudu — then yes, because the problem keeps coming back. For one wet holiday a year, probably not.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What’s the difference between waterproof and water-resistant socks?",
            "answer": "<p>A waterproof sock has a sealed layer inside it and stops water. A water-resistant sock has a treated or tightly knitted surface that shrugs off light rain for a while and then wets through. If a product doesn’t name a waterproof layer, it’s usually the second kind.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Are waterproof socks warm?",
            "answer": "<p>Warmer than the same sock without the layer, because they block wind and stop your foot cooling as water evaporates. But they aren’t insulated. Waterproof and warm are two different things worth choosing separately.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can you wear waterproof socks every day?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. Wash them cool and air dry them between wears. Most people who wear them daily keep two or three pairs so there’s always a dry one ready.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Are waterproof socks breathable?",
            "answer": "<p>They’re breathable, not ventilated. The layer passes sweat outwards as vapour, which delays the point where your foot feels damp. On a hard, sustained effort you’ll produce more than it can move. That’s physics, not a fault.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do waterproof socks work in snow?",
            "answer": "<p>They keep melted snow out well and they block wind, which is most of what makes feet cold. They aren’t insulated though, so in properly cold weather you want them for the dryness rather than the warmth.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q7": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Are they suitable for swimming or the shower?",
            "answer": "<p>No. They’re built for water arriving from outside during normal use — rain, spray, puddles, wet ground — not for being under water. We’d rather say so than take the sale.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q8": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What are the best waterproof socks in the UK?",
            "answer": "<p>Judge them on four things you can actually check: is the waterproof layer named, is the number of layers published, is sizing based on foot length, and does the seller say what the socks won’t do. Most listings fail at least two.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
